<h3>How to read this table</h3>
<p>BWP plywood is the only board that beats HDHMR on moisture — but at 30–50% higher cost. MR plywood costs the same as HDHMR but loses on surface smoothness. MR MDF (or HMR MDF) is cheaper but underperforms on moisture and screw holding. Particle board is essentially never the right answer in 2026 except for the cheapest temporary work.</p>
<p>For 80% of Indian residential interior projects, HDHMR is the right choice for indoor furniture. BWP plywood remains the right choice where water contact is a real possibility — under-sink cabinets, bathroom vanities, balcony storage.</p>
<h2>05 — Use casesWhere HDHMR shines</h2>
<p>These are the eight applications where HDHMR is genuinely the best material available at its price point — not just an acceptable substitute.</p>
<h4>Modular kitchen carcasses</h4>
<p>The carcass (the box the cabinet is built around) is the perfect use case. Hidden from view, exposed to humidity, takes screws and hardware. HDHMR's density and moisture tolerance shine here, and its lower cost vs BWP saves serious money.</p>
<p>18mm recommended</p>
<h4>Kitchen shutters (with laminate)</h4>
<p>Laminate-finished kitchen shutters benefit from HDHMR's smooth, defect-free surface. No grain telegraphing through the lamination, clean edges that hold edge-banding well. Better than plywood for laminated shutters specifically.</p>
<p>16–18mm recommended</p>
<h4>Wardrobe shutters and panels</h4>
<p>Large wardrobe shutters are an ideal HDHMR application — wide, flat, paint-ready surfaces that benefit from the board's dimensional stability. Less likely to warp over years than MDF, smoother than plywood.</p>
<p>16–18mm shutters, 18mm side panels</p>
<h4>Bathroom vanity units</h4>
<p>For vanities away from direct water exposure (under-counter, side panels), HDHMR works well — better than MDF on moisture, cheaper than BWP. For the immediate splash zone around the basin, switch to BWP plywood.</p>
<p>18mm with sealed edges</p>
<h4>TV units and crockery units</h4>
<p>Dry indoor display furniture is where HDHMR's smooth paintable surface really pays off. PU finishes or veneers applied over HDHMR look factory-grade — no telegraphing of substrate grain, no surface imperfections.</p>
<p>18mm body, 25mm for floating shelves</p>
<h4>Wall panelling &amp; ceiling features</h4>
<p>Fluted, grooved, or routed wall panels benefit from HDHMR's machinability — CNC tools cut sharp profiles without chipping. The paint-ready surface accepts colour evenly across the full panel.</p>
<p>12–16mm depending on profile</p>
<h4>Built-in study and office furniture</h4>
<p>Library units, study tables, home office storage — dry indoor furniture that needs to look architectural. HDHMR with PU finish gives this a premium, joinery-free look at a sensible cost.</p>
<p>18mm primary, 25mm tabletops</p>
<h4>Children's room furniture</h4>
<p>Sturdy, paintable, takes hardware well, and significantly cheaper than plywood. Use a quality water-based paint or laminate over HDHMR for kids' rooms and it'll outlast the room theme.</p>
<p>16–18mm</p>
<h2>06 — HonestWhen HDHMR is the WRONG choice</h2>
<p>This is the section every other HDHMR guide skips. Every material has limits. Knowing HDHMR's limits — and respecting them — is what separates a 15-year kitchen from a 3-year disaster.</p>
<h4>Direct water-contact areas</h4>
<p>Behind kitchen sinks where water can pool, the splash wall behind a wash basin, balcony cabinets exposed to rain. HDHMR resists humidity well but is not waterproof. For these, BWP marine plywood is the right call.</p>
<h4>Outdoor furniture or exposed exteriors</h4>
<p>Garden seating, outdoor storage, balcony shelving in unprotected positions. HDHMR is engineered for indoor use only. Direct sun and rain will degrade the board within 12–18 months, no matter what finish you apply.</p>
<h4>Long unsupported spans</h4>
<p>A 6-foot wide HDHMR shelf with no support in the middle will sag visibly within two years. Use plywood for long shelf spans, or break the span with intermediate vertical supports.</p>
<h4>Heavy-load applications</h4>
<p>Storage units intended to hold 50+ kg per shelf — heavy crockery banks, library walls, gym equipment storage. HDHMR is dense but not as load-bearing as cross-bonded plywood. Use 19mm plywood for these.</p>
<h4>Furniture you'll move repeatedly</h4>
<p>HDHMR is heavy — a single 18mm sheet weighs around 36 kg. For movable storage carts, foldable furniture, or anything that gets carried up apartment stairs, lighter MDF or particle board is more practical despite its other limitations.</p>
<h4>Where you want the wood look exposed</h4>
<p>HDHMR has no natural wood grain — it's a uniform fibre board. If you want a "raw wood" or industrial look with visible grain, plywood with its visible cross-section or solid wood is what you want. HDHMR is meant to be covered with laminate, veneer, paint, or PU.</p>
<h3>The "BWP for under, HDHMR for above" rule</h3>
<p>The smartest spec we give clients for kitchens: BWP marine plywood for the under-sink cabinet and the row immediately above the stove (where steam and splashing are constant), HDHMR for every other carcass and shutter. The cost difference is real but small (about ₹4,000–6,000 on a 10ft kitchen), and the moisture protection where it matters is non-negotiable.</p>
<h2>07 — InvestmentHDHMR cost in India (2026)</h2>
