<h2>10 — Protect yourselfHow to spot fake or substandard HDHMR</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The "no green edge" test.</strong> Premium HDHMR (especially Action Tesa) is dyed green throughout — visible on every cut edge. A board sold as HDHMR with white or grey edges is either a lower brand or an outright substitute. This is the single fastest authenticity check.</li>
  <li><strong>The weight test.</strong> A genuine 18mm 8x4ft HDHMR board weighs roughly 35–37 kg. A fake or substandard board feels noticeably lighter — usually by 15–20%. If your carpenter can lift the sheet too easily, the density is wrong.</li>
  <li><strong>The thickness verification.</strong> Boards sold as 18mm should measure 17.8–18.2mm with a digital caliper. Some vendors sell 16mm boards as "18mm with tolerance" — saving 10–12% per board on cost, charging you the full 18mm price. Carry a caliper or ask your carpenter to verify.</li>
  <li><strong>The brand stamp check.</strong> Genuine branded HDHMR carries the manufacturer's stamp, batch code and dimensions printed on the board. No stamp = no traceability = no warranty claim if it fails later. Insist on a stamped board.</li>
  <li><strong>The screw test.</strong> Drive a 50mm screw into the cut edge of a sample board. Genuine high-density HDHMR holds the screw firmly with no crumbling around the entry. Lower density boards crumble or split — easy to feel even before the screw is fully driven.</li>
  <li><strong>The smell test.</strong> Cheap boards with high formaldehyde content have a sharp, chemical smell when freshly cut. E0/E1-certified premium HDHMR has minimal smell. Spend 30 seconds near the board after a fresh cut — your nose is reliable here.</li>
  <li><strong>The price warning.</strong> Real Action Tesa or Greenpanel 18mm HDHMR cannot be sold at ₹65/sqft in Bangalore in 2026 — manufacturing cost alone is higher. Suspiciously low prices mean substituted or lower-grade boards in genuine packaging.</li>
</ol>
<h2>11 — PracticalInstallation gotchas (the things carpenters miss)</h2>
<p>HDHMR's performance in your home depends as much on how it's installed as on the brand you bought. These are the seven installation details that separate a 15-year kitchen from one that fails in three years.</p>
<p>01</p>
<p><strong>Always seal the edges.</strong> HDHMR's vulnerability is at the cut edges, where the fibre core is exposed. Every exposed edge needs either PVC edge banding (2mm minimum) or hardwood lipping. Skip this and moisture creeps in within 12–18 months.</p>
<p>02</p>
<p><strong>Use the right screws.</strong> HDHMR holds standard wood screws well but performs significantly better with confirmat screws (Euro screws) for cabinet joinery — they're designed for engineered board densities. Don't let your carpenter use leftover plywood screws on HDHMR.</p>
<p>03</p>
<p><strong>Pre-drill before screwing near edges.</strong> Within 50mm of any edge, always pre-drill a pilot hole. HDHMR is dense enough that direct screwing near the edge causes splitting — a common cause of "edge damage" that gets blamed on the board.</p>
<p>04</p>
<p><strong>Hinge spacing matters more than usual.</strong> Standard hinge spacing on plywood (2 hinges per shutter under 1m height) is borderline on HDHMR shutters because of the higher board weight. For shutters above 600mm wide, always use 3 hinges minimum. For wardrobe shutters above 2m tall, use 4.</p>
<p>05</p>
<p><strong>Let the board acclimatise.</strong> HDHMR delivered fresh from a factory needs 24–48 hours in the installation environment before being cut and assembled. Skipping this causes minor dimensional movement after installation that shows up as shutter misalignment within weeks.</p>
<p>06</p>
<p><strong>Internal carcass moisture barrier.</strong> For under-sink or near-bathroom cabinets, ask for a sealant or aluminium foil layer applied to the carcass internals where plumbing passes through. This is not standard practice but it dramatically extends the life of the cabinet.</p>
<p>07</p>
<p><strong>Don't use HDHMR for back panels in wet areas.</strong> The thin 6mm back panels of kitchen and vanity carcasses, when made from HDHMR in a moist environment, can swell over years. Specify 6mm BWP plywood for the backs in kitchens and bathrooms — costs ₹15–20 more per sqft and adds 5+ years of life.</p>
<h2>12 — Over timeHow HDHMR ages — the 10-year reality</h2>
<p>Properly installed, finished and maintained HDHMR furniture should serve you 10–15 years before refurbishment. Here is what the actual decade looks like.</p>
<h3>Years 1 – 3</h3>
<p>Furniture looks brand-new. No movement, no swelling, no visible wear. Hinges feel tight, drawer slides run smooth. The board is essentially indistinguishable from premium plywood at this stage.</p>
<h3>Years 3 – 6</h3>
<p>Visible wear begins to appear on hardware (hinges, handles, slides) — the board itself remains stable. Edge banding may start to lift on heavily used shutters; if it does, replace the band rather than ignoring it (water entry through a lifted edge is the #1 cause of HDHMR failure).</p>
<h3>Years 6 – 10</h3>
<p>Hinges may need adjustment or replacement on heavy shutters (especially in kitchens). The board's surface laminate may show wear on high-touch areas around handles. Edges that weren't sealed properly start showing minor swelling — repairable, but a sign of installation quality.</p>
<h3>Year 10 onwards</h3>
<p>Time to evaluate. A well-maintained HDHMR kitchen at 10 years has typically held up structurally but looks dated. Most homeowners use this point to reskin the shutters with new laminate while keeping the carcass — a cost-effective refresh that adds another 5–7 years. Full replacement is usually delayed to year 12–15.</p>
<h4>The single biggest factor in HDHMR longevity</h4>
<p>Edge sealing. We've examined hundreds of HDHMR installations across Bangalore. The single difference between furniture that lasted 12+ years and furniture that failed at year 5 was whether the cut edges were properly sealed at installation. Brand mattered less than this.</p>
<h2>13 — Before signingThe HDHMR buyer's checklist</h2>
<p>Before you sign off on any HDHMR-based quote, walk through these eight items. Get clear, written answers for each — these are the difference between a kitchen that lasts a decade and one that doesn't.</p>
<p>01</p>
<p><strong>Brand specified in writing.</strong> The BOQ must name the brand (Action Tesa, Greenpanel, Century etc.) and the product line, not just "HDHMR board". Vendors who refuse to commit in writing have a reason.</p>
<p>02</p>
<p><strong>Thickness verified per application.</strong> 18mm for carcasses and main shutters; 16mm acceptable for smaller shutters; 6mm only for backs and partition panels. Thinner than this means cost-cutting.</p>
<p>03</p>
<p><strong>Edge banding type and thickness.</strong> 2mm PVC edge banding minimum, applied with hot-melt adhesive. 0.4mm edge banding is sub-standard and fails within 2–3 years.</p>
<p>04</p>
<p><strong>Where you've allowed BWP plywood instead.</strong> Confirm in writing where the spec changes — typically under-sink cabinets, bathroom areas, back panels in wet zones. This needs to be explicit in the BOQ.</p>
<p>05</p>
<p><strong>Hardware grade and warranty.</strong> Hinges, drawer slides and locks should be from named manufacturers (Hettich, Hafele, Ebco, Blum) with their own warranties. The board is only as durable as what's screwed into it.</p>
<p>06</p>
<p><strong>Finish specification.</strong> Brand and grade of laminate, or PU finish brand if applicable, with the number of coats. Vague "premium finish" descriptions hide cost-cutting.</p>
<p>07</p>
<p><strong>Warranty terms in writing.</strong> Expect 5–10 years on the board against manufacturing defects (Action Tesa offers 7 years on HDHMR), 2 years on hardware, and at least 1 year on workmanship. Verbal warranties are not warranties.</p>
<p>08</p>
<p><strong>Delivery and curing timeline.</strong> If your project uses PU or paint finishes over HDHMR, the units need 7+ days curing before installation. Vendors who promise full delivery in 10 days are skipping the cure.</p>
